<sn><md>#### 1. Create a New Build System in Sublime Text

Sublime Text uses build systems to run external programs like your compiler. To create a custom one for C :

1.  Open Sublime Text.
2.  Go to the menu **`Tools`** → **`Build System`** → **`New Build System…`** .
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/Programming/C/00.Overview/assets/sublime_build_system_scrnsht.png)

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 2. Add the Build System Configuration

A new file will open. Delete any placeholder content and paste the following JSON configuration :

```json
{
    "shell_cmd": "gcc -std=c17 -Wall \"${file}\" -o \"${file_path}/${file_base_name}\"",
    "file_regex": "^(..[^:]*):([0-9]+):?([0-9]+)?:? (.*)$",
    "working_dir": "${file_path}",
    "selector": "source.c",
    "variants":
    [
        {
            "name": "Run",
            "shell_cmd": "gcc -std=c17 -Wall \"${file}\" -o \"${file_path}/${file_base_name}\" &amp;&amp; \"${file_path}/${file_base_name}\""
        }
    ]
}
```

+++ Let's break down what this configuration does :

*   **`"shell_cmd"`**: This is the main **command that runs** when you press the build shortcut. It invokes `gcc`, uses the `-std=c17` flag for modern C standards, and enables common warnings with `-Wall`.
*   **`"file_regex"`**: This is a pattern that allows Sublime Text to **parse compiler error messages**. When you get an error, you can click on the error in the output panel, and it will jump you to the exact line of code causing the problem.
*   **`"working_dir"`**: This **sets the directory where the compilation command is executed**, ensuring file paths work correctly.
*   **`"selector"`**: This tells Sublime that **this build system is intended** for C source files.
*   **`"variants"`**: This **adds an option to run your program after a successful compilation** . The `"Run"` variant compiles the file and then executes the output program.
+++

</md></sn><sn><md>#### 3. Save the Build System

Save the file (e.g., `C.sublime-build`) in the suggested "User" directory . The name you choose will appear in the build system menu.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 4. Select Your New Build System

1.  Go back to the menu **`Tools`** → **`Build System`**.
2.  Select your newly created build system from the list (e.g., `C`).
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 5. Write and Run Your C Code

Now you're all set. Open or create a C file (with a `.c` extension) to use this build system .

+++ get code
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("Hello, C!\n");

    return 0;
}
```
+++

*   **Compile Only**: Press **`Ctrl+B`** (Linux) to build/compile your code .
*   **Compile and Run**: Press **`Ctrl+Shift+B`** to open the build menu and select the **`Run`** variant . The output of your compiled program will appear in the panel at the bottom of the Sublime Text window.

</stdio.h></md></sn><sn><md>#### Optional Enhancements

Once the basic setup is working, you can enhance your environment further:

*   **Use a Terminal for `stdin`**: If your program requires user input, the built-in output panel will not allow you to type. To fix this, you can create a variant that opens a real terminal . On Linux, you can modify the `"shell_cmd"` for a variant to something like:
    ```json
    "shell_cmd": "gcc -std=c17 -Wall \"${file}\" -o \"${file_path}/${file_base_name}\" &amp;&amp; gnome-terminal -- bash -c \"${file_path}/${file_base_name}; exec bash\""
    ```
*   **Install Packages for Autocompletion**: You can install the `Package Control` plugin to manage other extensions. Useful packages for C development include `C Snippets` for code templates and `LSP` (Language Server Protocol) for better code completion and error checking .

If you ever get a "command not found" error, it means the `gcc` command is not in your system's `PATH` that Sublime can see. This is usually resolved by launching Sublime Text from your terminal using the `subl .` command .</md></sn>